.TH IRQ_BIND 2
.SH NAME
irq_bind, irq_ack \- interrupts as messages, for drivers (SYS_IRQ_BIND, SYS_IRQ_ACK)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long irq_bind(int irq, long port);
long irq_ack(int irq);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B irq_bind
delivers interrupt
.I irq
to
.IR port ,
one of the caller's ports: when it fires, the kernel masks the line and a
thread waiting in
.BR ipc_recv (2)
gets a "token 0" message whose
.I w[0]
has bit
.B IRQ_BIT(irq)
set. Interrupts that come while no thread waits are kept and delivered
together.
.PP
Interrupt numbers: on x86-64, ISA IRQs 0-15 or I/O APIC lines (GSIs) 16
and up; on arm64, the GIC's shared lines ("SPI"
.IR n ,
what a device tree's "interrupts = <0 n flags>" says).
.I irq
may be or-ed with
.B IRQ_LEVEL
(a level-triggered, active-high line: a PCI device's interrupt as the
chipset routes it) and
.B IRQ_LOW
(with
.BR IRQ_LEVEL :
active low). Each bound line goes to a processor of its own, in turn.
.PP
A line may be shared by up to 4 drivers (PCI lines are). Each bound
driver gets every interrupt of the line, checks its own device, and must
answer
.B irq_ack
at once, even when the interrupt was not its device's and even while
idle: the kernel unmasks the line only when all of them have. Binding a
line to the same port again does nothing. A process's lines are released
when its port closes.
.PP
Only drivers (processes started with
.BR SPAWN_DRIVER )
may bind.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 on success, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
.RB ( irq_bind )
The caller is not a driver.
.RB ( irq_ack )
The line is not bound to one of the caller's ports.
.TP
.B EINVAL
The number is out of range or reserved by the kernel (MSI lines come from
.BR irq_msi (2)),
the port is not the caller's, or the line cannot be routed.
.TP
.B EBUSY
Four drivers share the line already.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR irq_msi (2),
.BR ipc_recv (2),
.BR map_phys (2),
.BR dma_alloc (2),
.BR sys_spawn (2)
